# stream-sorting — AI Agent Rules

## Project identity

`stream-sorting` is a streaming sort and a suite of operations that consume or produce sorted object-mode Readable streams. Headline op: disk-backed external merge sort (designed for billion-record inputs on a laptop). Built on `stream-chain` (`readableFrom`) and `stream-join` (`select`, `sortedInsert` — the k-way merge primitives). Part of the `stream-chain` / `stream-json` / `stream-join` family. **Node-only** — server / CLI; browsers are out of scope. Scope: anything that **requires or preserves sortedness**.

## Critical rules

- **ESM.** The project is `"type": "module"`. Use `import` / `export`; no `require()` / `module.exports`.
- **No transpilation.** Code runs directly.
- **Two runtime deps: `stream-chain` and `stream-join`.** Never add other packages to `dependencies`.
- **Node-only.** The package targets Node servers and CLIs; the disk-backed sort relies on `node:fs`, `node:os`, `node:path`. No web entry, no browser tests.
- **Object mode is always on.** Every output Readable forces `objectMode: true`.
- **Backpressure must be handled correctly** — yield through `readableFrom`; no buffering above the sort's run-buffer.
- **Sort is disk-backed by default.** External merge sort writes sorted runs to a temp directory; k-way-merges them on output. In-memory-only and sliding-window-approximate strategies were considered and rejected.
- **k-way merge reuses `stream-join`.** Compose `select` + `sortedInsert(lessFn)` from `stream-join`; do not reimplement.
- **Comparator API follows `Array.prototype.sort`.** `(a, b) => number`; negative if `a < b`, positive if `a > b`, zero if equal.
- **Do not modify or delete test expectations** without understanding why they changed.
- **Do not add comments or remove comments** unless explicitly asked.
- **Keep `.js` and `.d.ts` in sync.** Every `.js` carries `// @ts-self-types="./X.d.ts"` at the top; JSDoc lives in the `.d.ts` only.
- **Helpers under `src/utils/`.** Main components at `src/` root.

## Code style

- Prettier: 100 char width, single quotes, no bracket spacing, no trailing commas, arrow parens "avoid" (see `.prettierrc`).
- 2-space indentation.
- Semicolons are enforced by Prettier (default `semi: true`).

## Architecture quick reference

Planned components (in scaffold phase):

- `sort(stream, options)` — disk-backed external merge sort; `compare`, `memoryBudget`, `tmpDir`.
- `mergeJoin(streamA, streamB, options)` / `joinBy` — SQL-style key join of sorted streams; variants `inner` / `left` / `right` / `full`.
- `union` / `intersection` / `difference` — set operations on sorted streams.
- `mergeSorted` — sorted merge without dedup; the suite's foundational op.

## Verification commands

- `npm test` — run the full test suite (parallel workers)
- `node tests/test-<name>.js` — run a single test file directly
- `npm run test:bun` — run with Bun
- `npm run test:deno` — run with Deno
- `npm run ts-check` — TypeScript type checking
- `npm run js-check` — `tsc --allowJs --checkJs` over the JS sources
- `npm run lint` — Prettier check
- `npm run lint:fix` — Prettier write

## File layout

- Entry point: `src/index.js` + `src/index.d.ts`
- Main components: `src/<name>.js` + `.d.ts` (at `src/` root)
- Helpers: `src/utils/*.js` + `.d.ts`
- Tests: `tests/test-*.js` (ESM) + `tests/helpers.js` for shared utilities
- Wiki docs: `wiki/` (git submodule)

## When reading the codebase

- Start with `ARCHITECTURE.md` for the module map.
- `.d.ts` sidecars are the canonical API reference.
- The `tests/` files demonstrate every supported usage pattern.
- Wiki markdown files in `wiki/` contain detailed usage docs.
